Creates and edits .gcpak files, which are what the engine loads assets from. It compiles source files into assets:

    .vert .frag .comp     GLSL shader, compiled to SPIR-V
    .png .jpg .jpeg       texture. Either sRGB (colors) or linear (data), see below
    .obj                  mesh (triangles only, with texture coordinates and normals)
    .gltf .glb            scene, compiled into a prefab. See below
    .hdr                  HDR panorama of a sky, compiled into the cube maps of a skybox and its lighting. See SKIES below

Assets are named after the file they came from, including its extension (e.g. "bricks.png"), whatever directory it was in.
Along with every .gcpak file, a .txt file with the names of the assets is saved. The editor needs it to open the .gcpak file again.

A texture asset says whether it is sRGB encoded. Image files are taken to be colors, and so sRGB, unless the file's name
(without the extension) ends with one of these words after a '-', '_' or '.':
    normal orm roughness metallic metalness occlusion ao height displacement mask linear
So "bricks-albedo.png" is sRGB, and "bricks-normal.png" and "floor_orm.png" are linear.

THE EDITOR

    gcpak_editor [FILE.gcpak | SOURCE FILE | DIRECTORY]

Press F10 to use the mouse. Package files, source files and directories can also be dropped on the window.

    Open New File                     shows the assets of another package file as well
    Add Source Files                  compiles the files and adds the resulting assets
    Add All Source Files In Folder    the same, for every source file in a directory and the directories inside it
    Save All To Package File          saves every asset that is listed into one .gcpak file

Select an asset to see what is in it. Textures, meshes and prefabs are drawn. An asset that is added replaces any asset with
the same name. The editor only loads content/shaders.gcpak itself, so that the other package files can be written to.

FROM THE COMMAND LINE

No window is opened, so this also works before content/shaders.gcpak exists.

    gcpak_editor --pack PATH... [--out FILE.gcpak] [--append] [--no-recurse]
    gcpak_editor --list FILE.gcpak
    gcpak_editor --bake-shadows PREFAB FILE.gcpak... [--resolution N] [--no-cast ENTITY]...
    gcpak_editor --bake-collision PREFAB FILE.gcpak...

    --pack PATH...      Compile these source files. For a directory, every source file in it. Other files are ignored.
    --out FILE.gcpak    Where to save. Can be left out when packing a single directory: content/textures is saved to
                        content/textures.gcpak
    --append            Keep the assets that are already in the output file. Assets with the same name are replaced.
                        Without this, the file is overwritten.
    --no-recurse        Don't look in the directories inside a directory
    --list FILE.gcpak   Print the assets in a package file
    --bake-shadows PREFAB FILE.gcpak...
                        Bake the shadow map of a prefab. See BAKING SHADOWS below
    --bake-collision PREFAB FILE.gcpak...
                        Bake the collision trees of a prefab. See BAKING COLLISION below

The exit code is zero if every file compiled. The package file is still saved if some didn't.
(On Windows the editor isn't a console program. Its output shows up in the console it was started from, but the console doesn't
wait for it to finish unless it is started with "start /wait", or the output is redirected.)

To package the engine's content, from the directory gcpak_editor is in:

    gcpak_editor --pack content/shader_src --out content/shaders.gcpak
    gcpak_editor --pack content/textures
    gcpak_editor --pack content/meshes

GLTF FILES

The default scene of a glTF file becomes a prefab, named after the file ("robot.glb"), that gc::loadPrefab() can load.
Prefabs don't contain resources, so the meshes, materials and textures that the scene uses become assets of their own, named
"robot.glb/mesh0_0", "robot.glb/material0", "robot.glb/texture0" and so on. They have to be in a package file that the game
loads, but not necessarily the same one as the prefab.

  - Every node becomes an entity, with the same name, transform and parent. They are all under a root entity (named after the
    file without its extension) which rotates glTF's Y-up coordinates to the engine's Z-up.
  - A node with a mesh gets a RenderableComponent, and a ColliderComponent with the same mesh, so that it is solid. If the mesh has several primitives, each one gets a child entity instead.
    Only triangle lists are supported. Normals and tangents are generated if the file doesn't have them. A primitive with too
    many vertices for one mesh asset is split up.
  - Materials are made from the base color (sRGB), metallic-roughness, occlusion and normal textures (linear). Constant factors
    are baked into the textures. A material without a texture gets no texture asset for it: the factors are stored in the
    material and the engine draws it with a simpler shader. The alpha modes become the engine's blend modes: MASK is alpha tested (cut
    out) and BLEND is alpha blended (see-through). The file's alpha mode is checked against the alpha that the material really
    has, as exporters often mark solid materials as BLEND: a material with no transparency is made opaque, and a BLEND material
    whose alpha is a cut out is made alpha tested: one where less than a quarter of the texture is partly transparent (fences),
    or where at least a tenth of it is holes (leaves, whose textures are mostly empty with soft edges). Emission is the emissive texture multiplied by the emissive factor (and by
    KHR_materials_emissive_strength, if the file has it). Double sided materials and texture transforms aren't supported.
  - Perspective cameras become CameraComponents that aren't active. Lights (KHR_lights_punctual) become LightComponents:
    directional and point lights, with their color, range and intensity. The engine uses glTF's physical units (lux and
    candela), so nothing is converted. A light without a range has no limit. Spot lights become point lights.
  - If the scene has a directional light, its shadows are baked, as --bake-shadows does for any prefab. See below.
  - The collision trees of the scene's meshes are baked, as --bake-collision does for any prefab. See below.
  - Animations, skins and morph targets are left out.

SKIES

A .hdr file is taken to be a panorama of a whole sky (equirectangular: twice as wide as it is high, with the horizon across the
middle), as HDRI sites provide. It becomes the three cube maps that a gc::SkyboxComponent uses, named after the file:

    "evening.hdr"              the sky as it is seen behind the world. As sharp as the panorama (up to 2048x2048 a face, 96 MB)
    "evening.hdr/irradiance"   the light that a surface facing each direction gets from the whole sky, for diffuse surfaces
    "evening.hdr/specular"     the sky as it is reflected by surfaces of each roughness, in six mip levels: sharp to blurry

The last two are what lights the world (image based lighting). They are worked out here so that the engine only has to read
them. The sun is left out of them, though it stays in the first: texels that are far brighter than the rest of the sky (more
than 8 times what 99% of the sky is below) are dimmed to match it. A world's sun is a directional light, which has shadows, and
it shouldn't light everything a second time through the sky. Where the sun was, and how its light compares to the rest of the
sky's, is stored in the sky's asset and printed, so that a game can match its directional light to it. The engine can turn the
sky so that its sun is over the directional light (SkyboxComponent::setFollowSun(), which is on by default).

The panorama's +Y is up, and is turned to the engine's +Z. The cube maps store what the file has: relative brightness, in a
high dynamic range format. How bright the sky is in the world, in lux, is set on the SkyboxComponent.

The games' skies are in content/textures/skyboxes, so they are part of content/textures.gcpak. Baking one takes a few seconds.

BAKING SHADOWS

    gcpak_editor --bake-shadows PREFAB FILE.gcpak... [--resolution N] [--no-cast ENTITY]...

The engine only draws shadows that were baked into a shadow map beforehand. Any prefab that has a directional light
(a LightComponent of type DIRECTIONAL) can be given one, however the prefab was made: from a glTF file, by a tool that uses
gc::PrefabWriter, or saved from a World with gc::savePrefab(). ember_court's map is made this way (see ember_court/README).

PREFAB is the name of the prefab's asset. It has to be in one of the package files. The meshes, materials and textures that it
uses are looked for in all of them, so list every package file that the prefab's resources are in. A mesh that isn't found
(such as one that the game generates when it starts) casts no shadow, with a warning.

The shadows that the prefab's meshes cast in the light are baked into an asset named "PREFAB/shadowmap" (4096x4096, 32 MB,
unless --resolution says otherwise). The prefab is given a root entity named "shadow_map" with a ShadowMapComponent that uses
it. Both are saved into the package file that the prefab was in. Baking the same prefab again replaces them.

    --resolution N      The width and height of the shadow map in texels. The default is 4096
    --no-cast ENTITY    The entity with this name, and everything under it, casts no shadows. For things that are drawn but
                        aren't really there, such as a sky. Can be given several times

The engine draws the shadows on everything, but only the prefab's own meshes cast them, and only for the direction that the
light had when they were baked. The holes of alpha tested materials don't cast shadows. Alpha blended materials cast a full
shadow where their alpha is at least 0.5 and none elsewhere. Renderables that aren't visible cast none.

    gcpak_editor --bake-shadows ember_court.map content/ember_court.gcpak --no-cast beacon

BAKING COLLISION

    gcpak_editor --bake-collision PREFAB FILE.gcpak...

The engine finds what a ray or a sphere hits in a collider's mesh with a tree of bounding boxes around the mesh's triangles
(a BVH). It can build that tree when a collider with the mesh first appears, but for a whole world that is a noticeable pause
when it is loaded, so the trees can be built here instead. As with shadows, any prefab can be baked, however it was made.

PREFAB and the package files are as for --bake-shadows. The tree of every mesh that one of the prefab's ColliderComponents uses
is put into one asset named "PREFAB/bvh". The prefab is given a root entity named "collision_bvh" with a CollisionBvhComponent
that uses it. Both are saved into the package file that the prefab was in. Baking the same prefab again replaces them.
A mesh that isn't found is left out, with a warning, and has its tree built by the engine as before.

The game has to register gc::CollisionBvhComponent for the baked trees to be used. Nothing else about colliders changes: the
trees are in the space of their meshes, so the prefab can be put anywhere, its colliders can move, and colliders that aren't
from a baked prefab work alongside them. Any number of baked prefabs can be loaded at once. The asset has its own copy of the
positions of the meshes, so bake again if a mesh is changed (compiling a glTF file always does).

    gcpak_editor --bake-collision ember_court.map content/ember_court.gcpak
